<!--
  Unraid guest for liskov.

  NOT applied declaratively. Install with:
    sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system define hosts/liskov/unraid-guest.xml
  and thereafter edit the live definition with `virsh edit unraid`. libvirt is
  stateful, and the point of this phase is to hand-tune passthrough while
  experimenting; a generated domain would fight that. This file is the
  known-good starting point and the thing to diff against when the live
  definition drifts.

  Deliberately NOT set to autostart. A host that boots straight into launching a
  storage guest is a host you cannot safely reboot to debug. Start it by hand:
    sudo virsh start unraid
  Matches virtualisation.libvirtd.onBoot = "ignore" in configuration.nix.

  ══ THE THING THAT WILL BREAK FIRST ══
  The three <hostdev> PCI entries below reference bus ADDRESSES (01:00.0 etc),
  because libvirt has no by-ID form for PCI passthrough. Those addresses have
  been observed to shift on this board, which is precisely why the host binds
  vfio-pci by vendor:device ID instead (see modules/nixos/vfio.nix). So before
  starting the guest after any hardware change, re-check:

    lspci -nn | grep 1b21

  and fix the addresses here if they moved. Symptom of a stale address is
  libvirt refusing to start the domain, or (worse) passing through whatever
  device now sits at that address.
-->
<domain type='kvm'>
  <name>unraid</name>
  <description>Unraid 7.3.2, migrated from bare metal. Owns the array and cache pools via passed-through SATA controllers.</description>

  <!--
    24GB of the host's 32GB. Sized for the arr stack, qBittorrent and SABnzbd
    staying INSIDE the guest during evaluation, so this is not yet a pure
    storage appliance. Comes down as Docker workloads migrate to the host.

    currentMemory equals memory: no ballooning. A storage guest that can have
    memory pulled out from under its page cache mid parity check is not a
    configuration worth measuring.
  -->
  <memory unit='KiB'>25165824</memory>
  <currentMemory unit='KiB'>25165824</currentMemory>

  <!--
    6 of the E3-1230 v2's 8 threads, arranged as 3 real cores x 2 threads so the
    guest scheduler sees an honest topology. The remaining physical core (2
    threads) stays with the host for qemu's own emulator threads and the
    hypervisor itself.

    TODO(install): verify the host CPU numbering with `lscpu -e` before trusting
    the pinning below. The assumption is the usual Linux layout where cpu0-3 are
    the first thread of each core and cpu4-7 the second, making (0,4) (1,5)
    (2,6) (3,7) the sibling pairs. If this box enumerates differently the pins
    below will split cores and cost noticeable throughput on parity math.
  -->
  <vcpu placement='static'>6</vcpu>
  <cputune>
    <vcpupin vcpu='0' cpuset='0'/>
    <vcpupin vcpu='1' cpuset='4'/>
    <vcpupin vcpu='2' cpuset='1'/>
    <vcpupin vcpu='3' cpuset='5'/>
    <vcpupin vcpu='4' cpuset='2'/>
    <vcpupin vcpu='5' cpuset='6'/>
    <emulatorpin cpuset='3,7'/>
  </cputune>

  <os firmware='efi'>
    <!--
      q35 + OVMF rather than i440fx + SeaBIOS: PCIe passthrough is materially
      better behaved on q35, which matters when handing over three controllers.

      `firmware='efi'` uses libvirt's firmware autoselection rather than a
      hardcoded loader path. On NixOS this is important: an explicit
      /nix/store/... OVMF path would be invalidated by the next nixpkgs bump,
      leaving a domain that will not start for a reason with no obvious
      connection to the rebuild that caused it. Backed by
      virtualisation.libvirtd.qemu.ovmf in configuration.nix.

      ══ UNRAID FLASH BOOT ══
      Unraid's flash drive ships legacy/syslinux bootable. For UEFI it needs its
      "EFI-" folder renamed to "EFI". That rename is SAFE for the bare-metal
      fallback: it enables UEFI boot while leaving the syslinux legacy path
      intact, so the flash still boots the machine bare metal exactly as before.
      Verify that on the flash before first guest start.

      If OVMF cannot enumerate the flash through the passed-through ASM1042 (a
      known-finicky combination), the fallback is SeaBIOS: replace this whole
      <os> element with
          <os><type arch='x86_64' machine='pc-i440fx'>hvm</type><boot dev='hd'/></os>
      and accept the weaker PCIe passthrough behaviour.
    -->
    <type arch='x86_64' machine='q35'>hvm</type>
    <boot dev='hd'/>
  </os>

  <features>
    <acpi/>
    <apic/>
  </features>

  <!--
    host-passthrough so the guest sees a real Ivy Bridge, including aes-ni,
    which Unraid's encryption leans on. This machine is not part of any
    migration pool, so there is no live-migration compatibility to preserve.
  -->
  <cpu mode='host-passthrough' check='none' migratable='off'>
    <topology sockets='1' cores='3' threads='2'/>
  </cpu>

  <clock offset='utc'>
    <timer name='rtc' tickpolicy='catchup'/>
    <timer name='pit' tickpolicy='delay'/>
    <timer name='hpet' present='no'/>
  </clock>

  <on_poweroff>destroy</on_poweroff>
  <on_reboot>restart</on_reboot>
  <!--
    Do not auto-restart on guest crash. During evaluation a crash is the signal
    being measured; silently rebooting it destroys the evidence and risks
    repeatedly slamming the array.
  -->
  <on_crash>preserve</on_crash>

  <pm>
    <suspend-to-mem enabled='no'/>
    <suspend-to-disk enabled='no'/>
  </pm>

  <devices>
    <emulator>/run/current-system/sw/bin/qemu-system-x86_64</emulator>

    <!--
      NO <disk> entries, deliberately. Every disk this guest touches arrives via
      the passed-through controllers below, including the boot flash. Handing
      Unraid an emulated virtio disk alongside real controllers is how you end
      up with the array trying to adopt a phantom device.
    -->

    <!--
      ══ PCI PASSTHROUGH ══
      IOMMU group 1 (both endpoints, they cannot be separated):
        01:00.0  1b21:1166  ASM1166 6-port SATA
                            array (4x 12TB) + Cache (WD Blue 500GB) + Fastservices (240GB)
        02:00.0  1b21:1042  ASM1042 USB3
                            carries the Unraid LICENCE FLASH DRIVE. Passing the whole
                            controller rather than a <hostdev type='usb'> entry means the
                            flash keeps its real USB GUID, which is what the licence is
                            tied to. An emulated USB disk presents a synthetic GUID and
                            will not license.
    -->
    <hostdev mode='subsystem' type='pci' managed='yes'>
      <source>
        <address domain='0x0000' bus='0x01' slot='0x00' function='0x0'/>
      </source>
    </hostdev>
    <hostdev mode='subsystem' type='pci' managed='yes'>
      <source>
        <address domain='0x0000' bus='0x02' slot='0x00' function='0x0'/>
      </source>
    </hostdev>

    <!--
      IOMMU group 9, isolated:
        03:00.0  1b21:1064  ASM1064 4-port SATA
                            BX500 x2, MX100 (if healthy), 1 spare.

      TEMPORARY. This controller returns to the host once the Docker workloads
      migrate off Unraid. Removing it then is: delete this one <hostdev> block,
      and delete the matching "1b21:1064" line from homelab.vfio.pciIds in
      configuration.nix. Nothing else references it.
    -->
    <hostdev mode='subsystem' type='pci' managed='yes'>
      <source>
        <address domain='0x0000' bus='0x03' slot='0x00' function='0x0'/>
      </source>
    </hostdev>

    <!--
      Bridged onto br0 so the guest is indistinguishable from bare-metal Tower
      on the LAN: same DHCP reservation, same address, same NFS exports that
      memory-alpha mounts as tower.internal.

      TODO(install): set the MAC to the bare-metal Tower NIC's address, so the
      existing DHCP reservation hands it the same IP and tower.internal keeps
      resolving with no DNS change. This is the single most important line in
      this file for preserving the five minute fallback guarantee.
    -->
    <interface type='bridge'>
      <source bridge='br0'/>
      <mac address='00:00:00:00:00:00'/>
      <model type='virtio'/>
    </interface>

    <!--
      Serial console, so the guest is reachable with
        sudo virsh console unraid
      without needing graphics. Unraid's own webGUI is the normal interface, but
      when the array will not start you want a text console that does not depend
      on the network being right.
    -->
    <serial type='pty'>
      <target type='isa-serial' port='0'>
        <model name='isa-serial'/>
      </target>
    </serial>
    <console type='pty'>
      <target type='serial' port='0'/>
    </console>

    <!-- VNC on loopback only. Reach it by SSH tunnel from serenity:
           ssh -L 5900:127.0.0.1:5900 z@liskov.internal
         Never exposed on the LAN: it is unauthenticated console access. -->
    <graphics type='vnc' port='5900' autoport='no' listen='127.0.0.1'/>
    <video>
      <model type='qxl'/>
    </video>

    <memballoon model='none'/>
  </devices>
</domain>
